.TH GPU 3
.SH NAME
gpu \- the browser's GPU, by WebGPU
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B bind -a #γ /dev

.B /dev/gpu/clone
.BI /dev/gpu/ n /ctl
.BI /dev/gpu/ n /data
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
On p9web,
.B #γ
gives programs the GPU of the computer that runs the browser, as WebGPU has it,
for computing: buffers, compute shaders in WGSL, and their dispatch.
It knows nothing of what the shaders compute.
It is shaped as
.IR draw (3)
is.
.PP
Opening
.B clone
makes a session, a directory
.IR n ,
and reads as
.IR n .
The session lasts while any of its files is open.
The sessions share one WebGPU device, as the clients of
.IR draw (3)
share one screen, with the adapter's limits
and those of the features
.BR shader-f16 ,
.B subgroups
and
.B chromium-experimental-subgroup-matrix
that the adapter has.
A session's buffers, shader modules and pipelines are its own,
but a buffer it names another session of its user may attach, as
.IR draw (3)
names images.
The device's queue runs the sessions' work in the order their writes come.
.PP
Reading
.B ctl
gives the adapter, a line for each of
.BR vendor ,
.BR architecture ,
.B device
and
.BR description ,
then the limits that matter for computing, a line for each with its value, such as
.EX
	maxStorageBufferBindingSize 4294967292
	minStorageBufferOffsetAlignment 256
	minUniformBufferOffsetAlignment 256
	maxUniformBufferBindingSize 65536
.EE
among them the buffers' sizes and the alignments of a binding's offset,
then, where the adapter says them,
.B subgroupMinSize
and
.BR subgroupMaxSize ;
a line
.B subgroupMatrix
for each of the adapter's subgroup matrix shapes, with its component type, its result's type
and its M, N and K, where the device has that feature;
then a line
.B features
naming the features that the adapter has and each session's device asks for,
and a line
.B wgsl
naming the WGSL language features the browser has, such as
.BR packed_4x8_integer_dot_product .
A shader that uses a feature enables it, as in
.BR "enable f16;" .
Where there is no WebGPU, in Node or in a browser without it,
.B ctl
reads
.B none
and a write to
.B data
fails.
.PP
A write to
.B data
holds messages, one after another; a message may go on into the next write.
Each is a letter, then its numbers, each 4 bytes, little-endian, then its bytes.
An
.I id
names a buffer, module or pipeline of the session; the program chooses it, and it must be free.
.TP
.BI b " id size usage"
Allocate a buffer of
.I size
bytes, zero.
.I Usage
is WebGPU's
.B GPUBufferUsage
bits, such as 0x80,
.BR STORAGE ;
.B COPY_SRC
and
.B COPY_DST
are always added.
.TP
.BI w " id off n bytes\fR[\fIn\fR]"
Write
.I n
bytes into buffer
.I id
at
.IR off ,
both multiples of 4.
.TP
.BI c " src soff dst doff n"
Copy
.I n
bytes of buffer
.I src
from
.I soff
into buffer
.I dst
at
.IR doff ,
all multiples of 4.
.TP
.BI s " id n source\fR[\fIn\fR]"
Compile a shader module from WGSL.
An error in it fails the write with its line, column and text.
.TP
.BI p " id module n entry\fR[\fIn\fR]"
Make a compute pipeline of the module's entry point, with the bind group layout the shader declares.
.TP
.BI P " id module n k entry\fR[\fIn\fR] " "\fR(\fPm name\fR[\fIm\fR] \fIvalue\fR[8])[\fIk\fR]"
Make a pipeline as
.B p
does, with
.I k
of the shader's override constants set:
each
.I value
is a 64-bit IEEE number, little-endian, for the constant
.IR name ,
such as a workgroup's size.
.TP
.BI d " pipeline n id\fR[\fIn\fR] " "x y z"
Dispatch
.I x
by
.I y
by
.I z
workgroups of the pipeline, with buffer
.IR id [ i ]
at binding
.I i
of group 0.
.TP
.BI D " pipeline n \fR(\fPgroup binding id off size\fR)[\fIn\fR] " "x y z"
Dispatch as
.B d
does, with
.I n
bindings: buffer
.I id
from
.IR off ,
for
.I size
bytes or, if
.I size
is 0, to its end, at
.I binding
of
.IR group .
An offset is a multiple of the alignment
.B ctl
gives for its kind, storage or uniform.
A uniform buffer is one with
.BR UNIFORM ,
0x40, in its usage; one buffer of slots, each bound by its offset, gives each dispatch its parameters.
.TP
.BI r " id off n"
Copy
.I n
bytes of buffer
.I id
from
.IR off ;
the next reads of
.B data
give them.
.TP
.BI F " tag"
A fence: when the GPU has done the messages before it, the next reads of
.B data
give the 4 bytes of
.IR tag .
.TP
.BI N " id in n name\fR[\fIn\fR]"
If
.I in
is 1, publish buffer
.I id
as
.IR name ;
if 0, withdraw the name, which must be the buffer's.
A name is its user's, not the session's: only that user's sessions see it,
and another user may publish the same name.
It goes when the session that published it
closes or the buffer goes.
.TP
.BI n " id n name\fR[\fIn\fR]"
Make
.I id
the buffer published as
.IR name ,
which stays while any session holds it, though its maker frees it.
.TP
.BI f " id"
Free a buffer, module or pipeline.
.PP
A write's messages go to the GPU in order:
a write into a buffer after a dispatch waits for the dispatch.
The write returns when they reach the GPU's queue, before the GPU has done them,
so a program can make its next messages while the GPU works.
Reads of
.B data
give the bytes of the
.B r
and
.B F
messages, in the order asked, each once the GPU has done the messages before it,
and an empty read when no more are to come.
A read that waits for the GPU may be interrupted, and takes nothing:
the next read gives those bytes.
An error names the message and fails the write:
the messages after it are not done, nor any part of a message it leaves unfinished,
and the next write starts afresh;
the messages before it may have taken effect, in part or whole,
but its reads and fences give nothing.
.PP
A message's bytes, after its letter and numbers, are at most 64 MiB;
a larger buffer is written in several
.B w
messages.
A write fails if its reads would leave more than 64 MiB unread,
or its buffers, modules and pipelines would make more than 16384 in the session,
or its buffers, its own and those it attached, more than 2 GiB.
A device has at most 32 sessions at once; opening
.B clone
when it has them fails.
.SH EXAMPLES
Add 1 to each of 64 integers, as in C:
.IP
.EX
src = "@group(0) @binding(0) var<storage, read_write> v: array<u32>;\en"
	"@compute @workgroup_size(64) fn main(@builtin(global_invocation_id) i: vec3u) "
	"{ v[i.x] += 1u; }";
.EE
then write
.BI b " 1 256 128"\fR,
.BI w " 1 0 256 " ...\fR,
.BI s " 2 " ...\fR,
.BI p " 3 2 4 " main\fR,
.BI d " 3 1 1 1 1 1"
and
.BI r " 1 0 256"
in one write, and read 256 bytes.
.PP
APE's
.B libwebgpu
carries out WebGPU's C interface,
.BR <webgpu/webgpu.h> ,
in these messages, so a program written for Dawn, such as ggml's WebGPU backend, runs on
.BR #γ .
.SH SOURCE
plan9web's
.B k/src/kernel/gpu.ts
.br
.B /sys/src/ape/lib/webgpu
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.IR draw (3),
.IR zero (1)
.br
.B https://www.w3.org/TR/webgpu/
.br
.B https://www.w3.org/TR/WGSL/
.SH BUGS
A lost device ends every session that has it:
their writes fail, and so do their reads, fences among them,
that the GPU had not done.
A session opened after it asks the browser for a new adapter and device.
